{"type": "vertex_text", "round": 3, "text": "I have completed the source-fidelity preflight review. The core claims and description are well-supported by the inventor's disclosure. However, the figure briefs, which guide the creation of the drawings, introduce several specific geometric and structural details that are not present in the source materials. These unsupported assertions appear to be reasonable choices for a draftsman, but they constitute new technical information that is not attested by the inventor.\n\nSpecifically, the briefs define a \"web\" structure for the spacer frame, describe the condensate channel as being integrally formed in the shell floor, and specify the shape of the condensate discharge opening as a \"break in the outline.\" These details, while minor, represent an over-specification of the invention beyond what the inventor has provided. Each instance has been identified as a critical `disclosure_fidelity` finding. The provided fixes generalize the language in the figure briefs to align with the shape-neutral, functional descriptions given in the source disclosure, treating the specific visual choices as schematic depiction conventions rather than assertions of invention.\n\nThe remainder of the application, including the claims, detailed description, and formalities, is internally consistent and faithful to the source. The review is clean apart from the five findings detailed below."}
